 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, the haze in Singapore is quite bad**, with air quality ranging from **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy** across the island. \n\nAccording to the National Environment Agency (NEA), southeasterly winds have been blowing moderate to dense transboundary smoke haze from forest fires in Kalimantan and Sumatra toward Singapore. \n\n### Air Quality Readings\n* **24-hour PSI:** Ranges from **131 to 202** (*Unhealthy* to *Very Unhealthy*).\n  * **West:** 202 (Very Unhealthy)\n  * **Central:** 185 (Unhealthy)\n  * **East:** 178 (Unhealthy)\n  * **South:** 161 (Unhealthy)\n  * **North:** 131 (Unhealthy)\n* **1-hour PM2.5 Concentrations:** Ranges from **79 to 187 µg/m³**, placing Singapore in **Band 2 (Elevated) to Band 3 (High)**. \n  * **West:** 187 µg/m³ (High)\n  * **Central:** 135 µg/m³ (Elevated)\n  * **South:** 117 µg/m³ (Elevated)\n  * **East:** 116 µg/m³ (Elevated)\n  * **North:** 79 µg/m³ (Elevated)\n\n### Health Advisories\nBecause of the *Unhealthy* to *Very Unhealthy* conditions and *Elevated* to *High* PM2.5 levels, the NEA advises the following precautions:\n* **Healthy individuals:** Reduce or avoid prolonged, strenuous outdoor physical exertion. \n* **Vulnerable groups (elderly, pregnant women, children) & those with chronic lung/heart disease:** Minimise or completely avoid outdoor activities. Consider wearing an N95 mask if you must go outdoors during high PM2.5 periods.\n\n**Outlook:** Winds are forecast to gradually shift to blow from the south or south-southeast alongside incoming showers over the next couple of days, which may help reduce the amount of Kalimantan smoke drifting over Singapore. For real-time hourly updates before heading out, you can check the official NEA portal at [haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg) or the myENV app.",
